<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan de Oñate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1550–<a href="/years/1626">1626</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a> was a conquistador born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> who led settlers, soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> up the Camino Real in 1598 and founded the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo México</a>. He became its first governor, but his brutal punishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo">Acoma Pueblo</a> in 1599 and costly failures led to his recall. He was later convicted of mistreating colonists and natives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate.jpg" alt="Juan de Oñate" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A d v a n c e d S o u r c e P r o d u c t i o n s Advanced Source Productions · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Equestrian_statue_of_Juan_de_O%C3%B1ate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a> was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>, the son of the Spanish-Basque conquistador and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> baron Cristóbal de Oñate. His mother, Catalina Salazar y de la Cadena, was descended from conversos and was the daughter of Gonzalo de Salazar, who had helped govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. Oñate married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-isabel-island">Isabel</a> de Tolosa Cortés de Moctezuma, a granddaughter of Cortés and great-granddaughter of the Aztec emperor Moctezuma Xocoyotzin. On 21 September 1595 Viceroy Luís de Velasco chose him to colonise the northern lands, though his successor delayed the expedition to review its terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 30 April 1598 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a> claimed the lands beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and then followed the river north, setting up his government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohkay-owingeh">Ohkay Owingeh</a>, which he renamed San Juan de los Caballeros. He granted land to colonists and let them demand tribute from the Native peoples. In October 1598 eleven Spaniards, among them his nephew Juan de Zaldívar, were killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo">Acoma Pueblo</a> in a fight over food. In January 1599 Vicente de Zaldívar destroyed the pueblo, and an estimated 800 to 1,000 Ácoma died. Oñate sentenced the survivors over 12 to twenty years of servitude and ordered a foot cut from each man over 25, though recent research suggests at most toes were cut.</p><p>In 1601 he led 130 soldiers and 12 Franciscan priests onto the Great Plains in search of Quivira, met the Escanjaques and the Rayados near the Arkansas River, and turned back after a battle with the Escanjaques, reaching San Juan on 24 November 1601. In 1604 and 1605 a party of about three dozen men went west and descended the Colorado River to the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1606 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a> was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. He resigned and was convicted of cruelty to natives and colonists, banished from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> for life and exiled from Mexico City for five years. He later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where the king made him head of all mining inspectors, and he died there in 1626.</p><p>He remains a disputed figure, honoured by some as an explorer and condemned by others for his cruelty at Acoma. On 29 December 1997 the right foot was cut from his statue in Alcalde, which was removed in 2020, and an equestrian statue unveiled in El Paso in 2007 drew protests from the Ácoma.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan de Oñate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1598 <b>Juan de Oñate</b> led settlers, soldiers and Franciscans north and founded the colony of New Mexico; after Acoma’s warriors killed a party of his soldiers, his forces stormed the mesa in January 1599 and killed several hundred of its people.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Juan de Oñate</b> wrote it first in 1598, and its origin is uncertain: the most widely accepted theory derives it from the Zuni word ʔa·paču, &quot;Navajos&quot;, another from Yavapai ʔpačə, &quot;enemy&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/culture/apache">Apache · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1598 <b>Juan de Oñate</b> led settlers, soldiers and Franciscans up the Camino Real from Mexico and claimed the upper Rio Grande, setting up headquarters at the Tewa pueblo of Ohkay Owingeh.</blockquote><a href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pánuco Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in Guadalcanal, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.98,-102.46&amp;year=1599" aria-label="Pánuco Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1812 1316 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan de Oñate"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2262 1586h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2262 1586h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.98,-102.46&amp;year=1599">What was here in 1599? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
